Section: Keywords and Sanitization

  zig-keywords : List Text =
   ["addrspace", "align", "allowzero", "and", "anyframe",
    "anytype", "asm", "async", "await", "break",
    "callconv", "catch", "comptime", "const", "continue",
    "defer", "else", "enum", "errdefer", "error",
    "export", "extern", "false", "fn", "for",
    "if", "inline", "linksection", "noalias", "nosuspend",
    "null", "opaque", "or", "orelse", "packed",
    "pub", "resume", "return", "struct", "suspend",
    "switch", "test", "threadlocal", "true", "try",
    "undefined", "union", "unreachable", "var", "volatile",
    "while"]

  is-zig-keyword-loop : List Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  is-zig-keyword-loop (kws) (name) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then False
   else if list-at kws i == name then True
   else is-zig-keyword-loop kws name (i + 1) len

  is-zig-keyword : Text -> Boolean
  is-zig-keyword (name) = is-zig-keyword-loop zig-keywords name 0 (list-length zig-keywords)

 Zig refuses any declaration named like a primitive type, not just
 keywords, and the x86 chapters bind names like f32 and i1 freely.
 A quoted @"f32" is accepted where a bare f32 is not, so primitives
 take the same rendering keywords already do. The integer forms are a
 family, not a list: i or u followed by digits, any width.

  zig-primitive-names : List Text =
   ["bool", "void", "type", "anyerror", "anyopaque", "noreturn",
    "isize", "usize", "comptime_int", "comptime_float",
    "f16", "f32", "f64", "f80", "f128",
    "c_char", "c_short", "c_ushort", "c_int", "c_uint",
    "c_long", "c_ulong", "c_longlong", "c_ulonglong", "c_longdouble"]

  zig-int-primitive-scan : Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  zig-int-primitive-scan (s) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then True
   else let c = char-code-at s i
   in if (c >= char-code '0') & (c <= char-code '9') then zig-int-primitive-scan s (i + 1) len
   else False

  is-zig-int-primitive : Text -> Boolean
  is-zig-int-primitive (s) =
   let len = text-length s
   in if len < 2 then False
   else let c0 = char-code-at s 0
   in if (c0 == char-code 'i') | (c0 == char-code 'u') then zig-int-primitive-scan s 1 len
   else False

  is-zig-primitive : Text -> Boolean
  is-zig-primitive (s) =
   is-zig-keyword-loop zig-primitive-names s 0 (list-length zig-primitive-names)
   | is-zig-int-primitive s

 The prelude declares these at file scope, and zig refuses a local that
 shadows a file-scope declaration. Quoting cannot help -- @"std" and std
 are the same identifier -- so these RENAME instead. X86_64Boot binds a
 chain st, sta, stb, stc, std, ste; the sixth one is `std`.

 Zig forbids a local shadowing a container-level declaration as well, so a
 name the prelude uses PRIVATELY is reserved for the whole program just as
 firmly as one it declares. That half is why this list is long and why it
 cannot be maintained by eye: `build/check-zig-prelude-surface.ps1` derives
 the surface from emitted output, anchored on zig-postlude-banner and reading
 to the end of the file, and counts parameters and captures as well as const
 and var. It refuses when a derived name is missing here. Run it after any
 change to zig-prelude.

 IT NOW COUNTS FUNCTION NAMES TOO, AND UNTIL 2026-08-28 IT DID NOT. The regex
 that harvests parameters reads past `fn NAME` to reach the parameter list and
 dropped the name on the way, so of the prelude's 96 declarations -- 74 fn and
 22 const/var -- this list covered the 22 and none of the 74. A zig file is a
 struct and its top-level declarations are its members, so a Codex program
 with a top-level named `cx-print` emitted a second `fn cx_print` and the file
 would not compile: `error: duplicate struct member name`. Finding 67, with
 both arms reproduced.

 Most of the 74 are `cx_`-prefixed, which is effectively this plug's namespace
 and is why no corpus program ever collided. Five are not: CxList and
 CxFn1..CxFn4, the comptime type constructors. Codex type names are CamelCase,
 so `CxList` is a name a program can pick without any sense of trespassing,
 and that is the collision worth caring about.

 The list is the UNION over the whole prelude and stays that way. A future
 that emits only the parts a program reaches must not narrow it to the parts
 that program got: the cost of keeping a name reserved for a program that
 never sees it is one spurious underscore, and the cost of narrowing is that
 the same Codex source emits a different spelling depending on which builtins
 it happened to touch.

  zig-prelude-decls : List Text = ["std", "main", "cx_entry", "cx_heap_mem", "cx_heap_reserve",
   "cx_heap_vtable", "cx_hp", "cx_gpa", "cx_dptr", "cx_bivy", "cx_nest",
   "cx_deck_base", "cx_deck_hw", "cx_deck_top", "cx_deck_best",
   "cx_deck_stride", "cx_deck_armed", "cx_deck_slack", "CxFn1", "CxFn2",
   "CxFn3", "CxFn4", "CxList", "cx_ll_empty", "cx_ll_push", "cx_ll_of",
   "cx_ll_concat", "cx_ll_cons", "cx_ll_insert_at", "cx_ll_with_capacity",
   "cx_text_compare", "cx_text_to_double_bits", "cx_bits_to_real_approx",
   "cx_char_to_text", "cx_char_encode", "cx_list_len", "cx_list_at",
   "cx_text_len", "cx_char_at", "cx_text_dup", "cx_substring", "cx_ipow",
   "cx_shl", "cx_shr", "cx_heap_base", "cx_buf_want", "cx_frontier_crosses",
   "cx_bump_alloc", "cx_bump_resize", "cx_bump_remap", "cx_bump_free",
   "cx_heap_save", "cx_heap_advance", "cx_heap_restore", "cx_peek_qword",
   "cx_address_of", "cx_deck_report", "cx_deck_enter", "cx_deck_exit",
   "cx_deck_pos", "cx_deck_set", "cx_utf8_to_cce", "cx_read_file_uni",
   "cx_write_all", "cx_write_binary", "cx_write_binary_buf", "cx_vtag",
   "cx_buf_write_byte", "cx_buf_write_bytes", "cx_peek_byte",
   "cx_buf_read_bytes", "cx_shru", "cx_mod", "cx_text_eq", "cx_is_letter",
   "cx_is_digit", "cx_new", "cx_concat", "cx_text_starts_with",
   "cx_text_contains", "cx_text_concat_list", "cx_list_set_at",
   "cx_text_to_integer", "cx_text_replace", "cx_text_split", "cx_cce_to_cp",
   "cx_cp_to_cce", "cx_cce_frame", "cx_cce_to_utf8", "cx_show_int",
   "cx_print_line", "cx_print", "a", "A", "acc", "al", "alignment", "ascii",
   "b", "B", "b0", "base", "bits", "bot", "buf", "bytes", "c", "C",
   "cce_t1_code", "cce_t1_size", "cce_t1_uni", "cce_t2_size", "cce_t2_uni",
   "cce_table", "chunk", "code", "cp", "ctx", "cx_a", "cx_acc", "cx_ap",
   "cx_b", "cx_base", "cx_ch", "cx_e", "cx_i", "cx_j", "cx_l", "cx_out",
   "cx_p", "cx_pa", "cx_pi", "cx_s", "cx_tail", "cx_tmp", "cx_used", "cx_v",
   "d", "D", "dot", "e", "fd", "frac", "h", "i", "k", "l", "len", "m",
   "memory", "n", "neg", "new_len", "o", "off", "out", "p", "path",
   "path_cce", "r", "R", "ra", "raw", "rc", "s", "sep", "size", "start", "T",
   "top", "u", "uni", "v", "vs", "z"]

  is-zig-prelude-decl : Text -> Boolean
  is-zig-prelude-decl (s) =
   is-zig-keyword-loop zig-prelude-decls s 0 (list-length zig-prelude-decls)

 A zig identifier is ASCII: letters, digits and underscore, or any
 string at all inside @"...". Codex names may carry the Tier-0 letters
 (CCE 97..127, `café`) and the lexer accepts them, so a name is made
 ASCII before anything else looks at it -- transliterated, not quoted,
 because the prefixed names below splice it into a longer identifier
 where a quote cannot sit. The codes are CCE: 3..12 are the digits and
 13..64 the ASCII letters, so the ASCII-identifier set is one band plus
 the underscore, and any other code becomes `_<code>`. Most names are
 already ASCII; the scan says so without rebuilding them. Finding 35.

  zig-ident-code-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  zig-ident-code-ok (c) =
   ((c >= 3) & (c <= 64)) | (c == char-code '_')

  zig-ident-is-ascii : Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  zig-ident-is-ascii (s) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then True
   else if zig-ident-code-ok (char-code-at s i) then zig-ident-is-ascii s (i + 1) len
   else False

  zig-ident-rebuild : Text, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  zig-ident-rebuild (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let c = char-code-at s i
   in if zig-ident-code-ok c then zig-ident-rebuild s (i + 1) len (acc & char-to-text (char-at s i))
   else zig-ident-rebuild s (i + 1) len (acc & "_" & integer-to-text c)

  zig-ascii-ident : Text -> Text
  zig-ascii-ident (s) =
   let len = text-length s
   in if zig-ident-is-ascii s 0 len then s
   else zig-ident-rebuild s 0 len ""

  zig-sanitize : Text -> Text
  zig-sanitize (name) =
   let s0 = text-replace name "-" "_"
   in let s = zig-ascii-ident (text-replace s0 "/" "_")
   in if is-zig-prelude-decl s then s & "_"
      else if is-zig-keyword s | is-zig-primitive s then "@\"" & s & "\""
      else s

 Names built by prefixing (_arg_, _lam<d>_, _v<d>_) must NOT go through
 zig-sanitize: a quoted @"f32" inside a longer identifier is not zig.
 The prefix already guarantees no keyword or primitive collision, so
 the raw replacement is all they need.

  zig-raw-ident : Text -> Text
  zig-raw-ident (name) =
   let s0 = text-replace name "-" "_"
   in zig-ascii-ident (text-replace s0 "/" "_")

 Field names arrive from the IR as name/index pairs like errors/5; the
 index restates what the name already says, so accessors drop it.

  zig-slash-pos : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  zig-slash-pos (s) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then -1
   else if char-code-at s i == char-code '/' then i
   else zig-slash-pos s (i + 1) len

  zig-field-name : Text -> Text
  zig-field-name (f) =
   let p = zig-slash-pos f 0 (text-length f)
   in if p < 0 then zig-sanitize f
      else zig-sanitize (substring f 0 p)

  zig-plain-field-name : Text -> Text
  zig-plain-field-name (f) =
   let p = zig-slash-pos f 0 (text-length f)
   in if p < 0 then f
      else substring f 0 p

Section: Type Mapping

  ZigTypeEntry = record {
   codex-name : Text,
   zig-name : Text
  }

  zig-type-map : List ZigTypeEntry =
   [ZigTypeEntry { codex-name = "Integer", zig-name = "i64" },
    ZigTypeEntry { codex-name = "Real", zig-name = "f64" },
    ZigTypeEntry { codex-name = "Text", zig-name = "[]const u8" },
    ZigTypeEntry { codex-name = "Boolean", zig-name = "bool" },
    ZigTypeEntry { codex-name = "Char", zig-name = "i64" },
    ZigTypeEntry { codex-name = "Nothing", zig-name = "void" }]

  zig-lookup-type : List ZigTypeEntry, Text, Integer -> Text
  zig-lookup-type (entries) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length entries then ""
   else let e = list-at entries i
   in if e.codex-name == name then e.zig-name
      else zig-lookup-type entries name (i + 1)

 A generic type is emitted as a zig function from types to a type, and every
 reference passes its arguments, so `Maybe Token` becomes `Maybe(Token)`.
 Zig instantiates and memoises one concrete union per argument list, which is
 monomorphisation with zig keeping the books -- and every signature that
 mentions the type stays concrete, so a mistake is a compile error here
 rather than something inferred and silently different.

  zig-type-args : List CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Text
  zig-type-args (args) (scope) =
   if list-length args == 0 then ""
   else "(" & zig-type-args-loop args 0 scope & ")"

  zig-type-args-loop : List CodexType, Integer, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Text
  zig-type-args-loop (args) (i) (scope) =
   if i >= list-length args then ""
   else let one = emit-zig-type (list-at args i) scope
   in if i == list-length args - 1 then one
      else one & ", " & zig-type-args-loop args (i + 1) scope

  zig-tparam-params : List Name, Integer -> Text
  zig-tparam-params (ps) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ps then ""
   else let one = "comptime " & zig-sanitize ((list-at ps i).value) & ": type"
   in if i == list-length ps - 1 then one
      else one & ", " & zig-tparam-params ps (i + 1)

 Definitions and calls are both flattened -- `fn f(a, b) T`, `f(x, y)` --
 so a function TYPE has to flatten the same way. Emitting the curried
 `fn (A) fn (B) T` here made every higher-order value disagree with the
 function it actually named, which zig only noticed once the generic types
 made these signatures concrete enough to compare. Only the spine flattens:
 a parameter that is itself a function keeps its own shape.

 A function VALUE is a closure, not a function pointer. Codex applies
 partially -- a 4-argument function called with 2 yields something callable
 with the remaining 2 -- and a `*const fn` has nowhere to keep the arguments
 already supplied. So every function-typed position emits CxFnN: a captured
 environment plus a trampoline that takes it back. Calling one is
 `f.call(f.ctx, ...)`.

 The arities are bounded because the prelude declares them. Going past the
 end is a compile error rather than a quiet fall back to a pointer type,
 which would fail later and further away.

  zig-closure-type : Integer, Text, Text -> Text
  zig-closure-type (n) (params) (result) =
   if n < 1 | n > 4
   then "@compileError(\"closure arity " & integer-to-text n & " unsupported\")"
   else "CxFn" & integer-to-text n & "(" & params & ", " & result & ")"

  zig-fn-param-count : CodexType -> Integer
  zig-fn-param-count (ty) =
   when zig-fn-spine ty
    is FunTy (p) (fnrow) (r) -> 1 + zig-fn-param-count r
    is otherwise -> 0

  emit-zig-fn-type : CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Text
  emit-zig-fn-type (ty) (scope) =
   zig-closure-type (zig-fn-param-count ty) (zig-fn-param-types ty scope) (emit-zig-type (zig-fn-result ty) scope)

 ForAllTy is the TYPE-level quantifier and this emitter handled only the
 effect-level one. Every place ForAllEff is unwrapped below, ForAllTy has
 to be unwrapped too, and it was unwrapped nowhere: a quantified type had
 no arrows to count, could not be peeled, and had no zig rendering at all.
 lower-def in the compiler does `strip-forall-ty full-type` for exactly
 this reason -- the quantifier says which variables are bound, and the
 shape underneath is the type.

 It stayed invisible for eight rungs because the default IR pipeline
 inlines, and an inlined definition arrives with its quantifier already
 gone. Turning the inline passes off -- which Passes.codex says a plug
 emitting SOURCE must do -- is what let a polymorphic definition reach the
 plug still quantified, and sort-by was the first to arrive that way.

  zig-fn-spine : CodexType -> CodexType
  zig-fn-spine (ty) =
   when ty
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> zig-fn-spine inner
    is ForAllTy (fid) (inner) -> zig-fn-spine inner
    is ForAllEff (c) (inner) -> zig-fn-spine inner
    is otherwise -> ty

  zig-fn-param-types : CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Text
  zig-fn-param-types (ty) (scope) =
   when zig-fn-spine ty
    is FunTy (p) (fnrow) (r) ->
     let rest = zig-fn-param-types r scope
     in if text-length rest == 0 then emit-zig-type p scope
        else emit-zig-type p scope & ", " & rest
    is otherwise -> ""

  zig-fn-result : CodexType -> CodexType
  zig-fn-result (ty) =
   when zig-fn-spine ty
    is FunTy (p) (fnrow) (r) -> zig-fn-result r
    is otherwise -> zig-fn-spine ty

 A `let` bound to a bare integer literal is a `comptime_int` in Zig, and a
 closure that CAPTURES it takes `@TypeOf(k)` for the environment field, which
 is then a comptime-only type in a runtime struct: `cannot load comptime-only
 type 'comptime_int'`, six times on `lam-capture-two`. The annotation forces
 the runtime type at the binding, where the IR already knows it.

 Only the scalar types are annotated. A list, a closure or a record binds to an
 expression whose Zig type is spelled by construction, and annotating those
 would put this emitter's idea of the type in front of the value's own.

  zig-let-annot : CodexType -> Text
  zig-let-annot (ty) =
   when ty
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> zig-let-annot inner
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (mode) -> ": i64"
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> ": f64"
    is BooleanTy -> ": bool"
    is CharTy -> ": i64"
    is otherwise -> ""

 A unit family is integer-backed: `Length = unit family Millimeter` with
 scale factors, and a `Length` value IS its base-unit integer. The plug
 mapped every `UnitTy` to `void`, which erased the payload while the
 arithmetic around it stayed correct -- `unit-family`'s emitted body
 computes 2400, 240, 2 and 100, exactly its `.expected`, and the only
 wrong thing in the program is that `fn Centimeter(__fv: i64)` returns
 `void`, which makes `(w +% h)` an `invalid operands: void and void`.
 Finding 17, and this arm is half of it.

 The other half is not here: a record FIELD typed by a unit family
 arrives as an `ATypeExpr` and never reaches this function, so it is
 still emitted as the bare family name. That is 11 more programs and a
 path this has not touched.

 `zig-let-annot` peels too, or a `let` holding a unit value would be
 annotated `""` while its expression has an integer type. And the entry
 shim now recurses instead of assuming `void`, which is what bare metal
 does (`emit-opening-result-print` recurses into the inner type); it had
 been calling without printing, correct only while units were void.

  emit-zig-type : CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Text
  emit-zig-type (ty) (scope) =
   let stray = zig-stray-tvar ty scope
   in if stray >= 0 then zig-tvar-scope-refusal stray
   else when ty
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (mode) -> "i64"
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> "f64"
    is TextTy -> "[]const u8"
    is BooleanTy -> "bool"
    is CharTy -> "i64"
    is VoidTy -> "void"
    is NothingTy -> "void"
    is ProofTy -> "void"
    is PropEqTy (pl) (pr) -> "void"
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> emit-zig-type inner scope
    is ListTy (elem) -> "*CxList(" & emit-zig-type elem scope & ")"
    is LinkedListTy (elem) -> "*CxList(" & emit-zig-type elem scope & ")"
    is VectorTy (vn) (vt) -> "@Vector(" & show vn & ", " & emit-zig-type vt scope & ")"
    is VectorMaskTy (vn) -> "@Vector(" & show vn & ", bool)"
    is FunTy (p) (fnrow) (r) -> emit-zig-fn-type ty scope
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> emit-zig-type inner scope
    is ForAllTy (fid) (inner) -> emit-zig-type inner scope
    is ForAllEff (c) (inner) -> emit-zig-type inner scope
    is RecordTy (n) (a) (f) -> zig-sanitize (n.value) & zig-type-args a scope
    is SumTy (n) (a) (c) -> zig-sanitize (n.value) & zig-type-args a scope
    is ConstructedTy (n) (a) -> zig-sanitize (n.value) & zig-type-args a scope
    is TypeCon (n) -> zig-sanitize (n.value)
    is TypeVar (id) -> "T" & integer-to-text id
    is otherwise -> "@compileError(\"zig plug: no zig type for this codex type\")"

 A definition quantified over type variables becomes a zig function taking
 those types as comptime parameters, the same move generic TYPES already
 make. Zig will not accept anyopaque as a parameter type at all, so
 map-list : (a -> b), List a -> List b has nowhere to put a and b without
 this.

 CSharpPlug declares the equivalent and stops there, because C# infers a
 call's type arguments. Zig does not infer a comptime type parameter, so
 the call site has to name them, and naming them means recovering each
 variable from the types actually passed.

  zig-has-int : List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295), Integer between 0 and 4294967295, Integer -> Boolean
  zig-has-int (xs) (n) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then False
   else if list-at xs i == n then True
   else zig-has-int xs n (i + 1)

  zig-tvar-ids : CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295)
  zig-tvar-ids (ty) (acc) =
   when ty
    is TypeVar (id) -> if zig-has-int acc id 0 then acc else acc & [id]
    is FunTy (p) (fnrow) (r) -> zig-tvar-ids r (zig-tvar-ids p acc)
    is ListTy (elem) -> zig-tvar-ids elem acc
    is LinkedListTy (elem) -> zig-tvar-ids elem acc
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> zig-tvar-ids inner acc
    is ForAllTy (fid) (inner) -> zig-tvar-ids inner acc
    is ForAllEff (c) (inner) -> zig-tvar-ids inner acc
    is ConstructedTy (n) (args) -> zig-tvar-ids-list args acc 0
    is SumTy (n) (args) (c) -> zig-tvar-ids-list args acc 0
    is RecordTy (n) (args) (f) -> zig-tvar-ids-list args acc 0
    is VectorTy (vn) (vt) -> zig-tvar-ids vt acc
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> zig-tvar-ids inner acc
    is LinearTy (inner) -> zig-tvar-ids inner acc
    is PropEqTy (pl) (pr) -> zig-tvar-ids pr (zig-tvar-ids pl acc)
    is TypeApply (f) (a) -> zig-tvar-ids a (zig-tvar-ids f acc)
    is otherwise -> acc

  zig-tvar-ids-list : List CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295), Integer -> List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295)
  zig-tvar-ids-list (types) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length types then acc
   else zig-tvar-ids-list types (zig-tvar-ids (list-at types i) acc) (i + 1)

 A TYPE VARIABLE IS A ZIG COMPTIME PARAMETER AND NOTHING ELSE, so naming
 one is legal in exactly the bodies whose signature declared it. `scope` is
 that set -- what emit-zig-def put in as `comptime T<n>: type` -- and it now
 rides with every type this emitter renders. Where a variable is not in it,
 `T<n>` is a name zig has never heard of, and the answer is a refusal.

 This is the third patch of one shape, made once instead. Two sites learned
 the rule separately -- zig-resolve-tvar's last-resort answer and the
 closure environment struct -- and a third was queued behind them
 (a callee's variable emitted untranslated into a caller, `cx_ll_of(
 HamtEntry(T25), ...)` inside a function declaring T70). The emitter memory
 had already named the shape in advance: six sites had to learn independently
 that a generic name must be applied, expect a seventh. Threading the scope
 through emit-zig-type is what stops the counting.

 The refusal fires at the OUTERMOST type rather than at each variable inside
 it, which is not only tidier: zig-is-unmapped tests a leading @compileError,
 and a marker buried inside `*CxList(...)` would not be seen by the two
 callers that ask "could this type be rendered at all". One gap, one marker,
 at the front where the test can read it.

 A ForAllTy binder does NOT put its variable in scope. The quantifier is a
 codex-level fact; the zig declaration is the comptime parameter, and only
 emit-zig-def makes one.

  zig-stray-tvar : CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Integer
  zig-stray-tvar (ty) (scope) =
   zig-first-stray-tvar (zig-tvar-ids ty []) scope 0

  zig-first-stray-tvar : List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295), List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295), Integer -> Integer
  zig-first-stray-tvar (ids) (scope) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ids then 0 - 1
   else let id = list-at ids i
   in if zig-has-int scope id 0 then zig-first-stray-tvar ids scope (i + 1) else id

  zig-tvar-scope-refusal : Integer -> Text
  zig-tvar-scope-refusal (id) =
   "@compileError(\"zig plug: type variable T" & integer-to-text id & " is not declared at this site\")"

 Record fields and ctor payloads are declared as ATypeExpr in the type
 defs the plug receives, so they get their own mapping onto the same
 zig names emit-zig-type produces.

  zig-afn-param-types : ATypeExpr -> Text
  zig-afn-param-types (te) =
   when te
    is AFunType (p) (r) (s) ->
     let rest = zig-afn-param-types r
     in if text-length rest == 0 then emit-zig-atype p
        else emit-zig-atype p & ", " & rest
    is AEffectType (effs) (scopes) (tail) (ret) (s) -> zig-afn-param-types ret
    is otherwise -> ""

  zig-afn-param-count : ATypeExpr -> Integer
  zig-afn-param-count (te) =
   when te
    is AFunType (p) (r) (s) -> 1 + zig-afn-param-count r
    is AEffectType (effs) (scopes) (tail) (ret) (s) -> zig-afn-param-count ret
    is otherwise -> 0

  zig-afn-result : ATypeExpr -> ATypeExpr
  zig-afn-result (te) =
   when te
    is AFunType (p) (r) (s) -> zig-afn-result r
    is AEffectType (effs) (scopes) (tail) (ret) (s) -> zig-afn-result ret
    is otherwise -> te

  emit-zig-atype : ATypeExpr -> Text
  emit-zig-atype (te) =
   when te
    is ANamedType (name) (s) ->
     let mapped = zig-lookup-type zig-type-map (name.value) 0
     in if text-length mapped > 0 then mapped else zig-sanitize (name.value)
    is AAppType (base) (args) (s) ->
     when base
      is ANamedType (bn) (bs) ->
       if (bn.value == "List") | (bn.value == "LinkedList")
       then "*CxList(" & emit-zig-atype (list-at args 0) & ")"
       else zig-sanitize (bn.value) & zig-atype-args args
      is otherwise -> "@compileError(\"zig plug: no zig type for this applied type\")"
    is ABoundedIntType (b) (lo) (hi) (mode) (s) -> "i64"
    is AEffectType (effs) (scopes) (tail) (ret) (s) -> emit-zig-atype ret
    is AFunType (p) (r) (s) -> zig-closure-type (zig-afn-param-count te) (zig-afn-param-types te) (emit-zig-atype (zig-afn-result te))
    is ALinearType (inner) (s) -> emit-zig-atype inner
    is otherwise -> "@compileError(\"zig plug: no zig type for this type expression\")"

  zig-peel-return : CodexType, Integer -> CodexType
  zig-peel-return (ty) (n) =
   when ty
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> zig-peel-return inner n
    is ForAllTy (fid) (inner) -> zig-peel-return inner n
    is ForAllEff (c) (inner) -> zig-peel-return inner n
    is FunTy (p) (fnrow) (r) -> if n <= 0 then ty else zig-peel-return r (n - 1)
    is otherwise -> ty

  zig-type-is-void : CodexType -> Boolean
  zig-type-is-void (ty) =
   when ty
    is VoidTy -> True
    is NothingTy -> True
    is ProofTy -> True
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> zig-type-is-void inner
    is ForAllTy (fid) (inner) -> zig-type-is-void inner
    is ForAllEff (c) (inner) -> zig-type-is-void inner
    is otherwise -> False

 Every node that CARRIES a type answers with it, and the one that does not
 is the reason this list is long. IrLet holds the type of the name it
 BINDS, not of the expression: `let len = list-length xs in ...` would
 answer Integer, which is len's type and not the let's. A let is worth
 whatever its body is worth, so it recurses.

 Falling into otherwise means answering VoidTy, and void is a legitimate
 codex type rather than a marker, so a missing arm here is indistinguishable
 from a genuinely void expression. sort-by paid for that: its body is a let,
 the let answered void, and with an empty type-val on the definition the
 emitter had nothing better to fall back to, so it declared a function
 returning void around a body returning a list.

 IrLambda is missing ON PURPOSE and answering it costs five milestones. A
 lambda handed to map-list carries the CALLEE's type variables, so a caller
 asking "what type is this argument" gets `a -> b` in map-list's own
 numbering. Resolving map-list's `a` against that answers `a` with itself,
 and T26 reaches zig in a function that declares no such thing. Silence
 here is what makes the resolver skip the lambda and recover the element
 type from the list argument instead, which is the answer that works.

 The other type-carrying nodes are absent for a duller reason: nothing has
 needed them. A missing one shows up as a wrong void, loudly, the way this
 one did.

  zig-expr-type : IRExpr -> CodexType
  zig-expr-type (e) =
   when e
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> TextTy
    is IrIntLit (n) (sp) -> int-ty-default
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrFieldAccess (rec) (field) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrMatch (scrut) (branches) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrRecord (name) (fields) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrLet (n) (ty) (v) (b) (sp) -> zig-expr-type b
    is IrList (es) (ty) (sp) -> ListTy ty
    is otherwise -> VoidTy

  zig-is-text-type : CodexType -> Boolean
  zig-is-text-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is TextTy -> True
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> zig-is-text-type inner
    is otherwise -> False

 Integer arithmetic wraps: Integer is the 64-bit machine word, bare metal
 emits plain add/sub/mul/neg, and C#'s unchecked context does the same.
 Zig's +/-/* are overflow-checked, so the integer rows carry the wrapping
 forms and negation asks this predicate which family it is in.

  zig-is-real-type : CodexType -> Boolean
  zig-is-real-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> True
    is VectorTy (vn) (vt) -> zig-is-real-type vt
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> zig-is-real-type inner
    is otherwise -> False

 `show` is one name over five different jobs. `show : forall a. a -> Text`
 (`Types/Builtins.codex:69`), and bare metal picks the job from the
 ARGUMENT's type (`Emit/X86_64.codex:1652`): a Text shows as itself, a
 Boolean as `True`/`False`, an f32 widens before `__real_to_text`, any
 other real goes straight there, and everything else is `__itoa`. This
 plug emitted the last arm for all five, so 40 corpus programs failed
 with `expected type 'i64', found 'bool'` and 2 with `found 'f64'` --
 42 of 113 refusals, the largest single class. Finding 50.

 The unit wrapper comes off first, and bare metal's note says why it
 learned to: without the strip a `unit Text` misses the arm its inner
 type is entitled to, falls to `__itoa`, and prints the pointer as a
 decimal number. `unit Integer` was already right, which is what made it
 cheap to miss.

 Reals refuse rather than guess. `__real_to_text` on bare metal is
 hand-written assembly -- sign bit, `cvttsd2si` for the integer part,
 fifteen fractional-digit iterations, CCE digit offsets -- and no
 `cx_real_to_text` exists here. `std.fmt` would agree with it on some
 values and not others, and a `show` that is right for 2.5 and wrong for
 0.1 is worse than one that says it cannot. Two corpus programs want it.

 A `when` on a Boolean reaches the plug as an `IrLitPat` whose value is the
 SPELLING `True` or `False`, not a number. Bare metal decodes it through
 `pat-lit-to-integer` (`Syntax/Token.codex:149`), a shared compiler
 function whose whole body is that rule: `True` is 1, `False` is 0,
 anything else parses as an integer. This plug emitted the spelling
 straight into a zig switch label, and zig has no `True` -- it has `true`.

 `codex/test/when-bool-cross.codex` is a cross-backend regression that
 states the requirement in its own header, and it has been failing here
 since the plug was written. Finding 52.

 Bare metal's 1 and 0 are right for a machine word; a zig scrutinee of
 this shape is already `bool`, so the same rule spells out as `true` and
 `false`. It dispatches on the spelling rather than the type because that
 is what `pat-lit-to-integer` does, and a divergence there would be its
 own defect.

  zig-lit-pat-text : Text, CodexType -> Text
  zig-lit-pat-text (t) (ty) =
   when ty
    is TextTy -> t
    is otherwise -> zig-bool-pat-text t

  zig-bool-pat-text : Text -> Text
  zig-bool-pat-text (t) =
   if t == "True" then "true"
   else if t == "False" then "false"
   else t

  zig-strip-unit : CodexType -> CodexType
  zig-strip-unit (ty) =
   when ty
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> zig-strip-unit inner
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> zig-strip-unit inner
    is otherwise -> ty

  zig-is-boolean-type : CodexType -> Boolean
  zig-is-boolean-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is BooleanTy -> True
    is otherwise -> False

 Bare metal resolves a named type to its underlying form before it
 dispatches (`resolve-to-sum-with-defs`); this does not. A type alias
 whose right-hand side is Boolean still shows as an integer here. Named
 rather than left silent: no corpus program is in that shape, so it is
 an unmeasured gap and not a measured one.

  zig-show-arg : IRExpr, ZigCtx, Integer -> Text
  zig-show-arg (a) (ctx) (d) =
   let e = emit-zig-expr a ctx (d + 1)
   in let aty = zig-strip-unit (zig-expr-type a)
   in if zig-is-real-type aty then zig-show-real-refusal
   else if zig-is-text-type aty then e
   else if zig-is-boolean-type aty then zig-show-bool-text e
   else "cx_show_int(" & e & ")"

  zig-show-bool-text : Text -> Text
  zig-show-bool-text (e) =
   let t = "@as([]const u8, \"" & zig-escape-text "True" & "\")"
   in let f = "@as([]const u8, \"" & zig-escape-text "False" & "\")"
   in "(if (" & e & ") " & t & " else " & f & ")"

  zig-show-real-refusal : Text
  zig-show-real-refusal =
   "@compileError(\"zig plug: show on a Real needs __real_to_text, which this plug does not have\")"

Section: Arity Map

  ArityEntry = record {
   name : Text,
   arity : Integer between 0 and 255,
   generic-count : Integer between 0 and 255
  }

  bsearch-arity-pos : List ArityEntry, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bsearch-arity-pos (entries) (name) (lo) (hi) =
   if lo >= hi then lo
   else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
   in let mid-name = (list-at entries mid).name
   in if text-compare name mid-name <= 0 then bsearch-arity-pos entries name lo mid
      else bsearch-arity-pos entries name (mid + 1) hi

  sorted-insert-arity : List ArityEntry, ArityEntry -> List ArityEntry
  sorted-insert-arity (xs) (e) =
   let len = list-length xs
   in let pos = bsearch-arity-pos xs (e.name) 0 len
   in list-insert-at xs pos e

  build-arity-map : List IRDef, Integer, List ArityEntry -> List ArityEntry
  build-arity-map (defs) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length defs then acc
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in build-arity-map defs (i + 1) (sorted-insert-arity acc (ArityEntry {
    name = d.name,
    arity = __narrow (list-length (d.params)),
    generic-count = 0
   }))

  lookup-arity : List ArityEntry, Text -> Integer
  lookup-arity (entries) (name) =
   let len = list-length entries
   in let pos = bsearch-arity-pos entries name 0 len
   in if pos >= len then -1
      else let e = list-at entries pos
      in if e.name == name then e.arity else -1

Section: Constructor Map

  ZigCtorEntry = record {
   ctor-name : Text,
   type-name : Text,
   ctor-arity : Integer between 0 and 255,
   enum-like : Boolean
  }

 renamed-from and renamed-to are parallel: a name emitted inside a lambda
 body is looked up in the first and emitted as the matching entry of the
 second. Zig forbids a nested function's parameter from shadowing an
 enclosing one, and the desugarer produces exactly that -- `for p in pats`
 inside desugar-pattern(p) -- so the lambda's parameter is renamed and its
 body has to follow. A const alias does not avoid the rule; it reaches
 into the nested struct's function too.

 bound-names lists the codex names that are runtime locals of the zig
 function being emitted -- its parameters, its let bindings, its match
 binders. Top-level definitions are not in it and never need capturing.
 A lambda intersects this list with the names its body mentions, and what
 falls out is exactly what its environment has to carry, because zig lets
 a nested function reach a comptime constant but not a runtime value.

  ZigCtx = record {
   arities : List ArityEntry,
   ctors : List ZigCtorEntry,
   typedefs : List ATypeDef,
   irdefs : List IRDef,
   renamed-from : List Text,
   renamed-to : List Text,
   tvar-ids : List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295),
   tvar-types : List CodexType,
   expected : CodexType,
   bound-names : List Text,
   scope-tvars : List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295)
  }

 An empty list literal reaches the IR without a concrete element type,
 so emission recovers it from context: the declared field type when the
 literal initializes a record field, the callee's parameter type when
 it is an argument. No context falls back to i64, which fails loudly at
 zig compile time rather than silently coercing.

  zig-is-empty-list : IRExpr -> Boolean
  zig-is-empty-list (e) =
   when e
    is IrList (elems) (ty) (sp) -> list-length elems == 0
    is otherwise -> False

 A generic type is a function from types to a type, so naming one in a
 declaration has to apply it: a field of `Maybe Def` is Maybe(Def), not
 Maybe. Dropping the arguments here is what left record fields declared as
 the function rather than the type.

  zig-atype-args : List ATypeExpr -> Text
  zig-atype-args (args) =
   if list-length args == 0 then ""
   else "(" & zig-atype-args-loop args 0 & ")"

  zig-atype-args-loop : List ATypeExpr, Integer -> Text
  zig-atype-args-loop (args) (i) =
   if i == list-length args then ""
   else let one = emit-zig-atype (list-at args i)
   in if i == list-length args - 1 then one
      else one & ", " & zig-atype-args-loop args (i + 1)

 A field declaration is written in the RECORD's type parameters, and a
 construction site is not inside the record's declaration, so `a` there
 names nothing zig has declared. The site's own type arguments are the
 answer and they line up by position, the way zig-ctor-type-args renders
 them for the struct name on the same line. A variable this cannot place
 answers nothing rather than its own spelling: the caller's marker is a
 diagnostic and a bare `a` is an undeclared identifier.

  zig-atype-mentions : ATypeExpr, Text -> Boolean
  zig-atype-mentions (te) (n) =
   when te
    is ANamedType (nm) (s) -> nm.value == n
    is AAppType (base) (args) (s) -> zig-atype-mentions base n | zig-atype-list-mentions args 0 n
    is AEffectType (effs) (scopes) (tail) (ret) (s) -> zig-atype-mentions ret n
    is AFunType (p) (r) (s) -> zig-atype-mentions p n | zig-atype-mentions r n
    is ALinearType (inner) (s) -> zig-atype-mentions inner n
    is otherwise -> False

  zig-atype-list-mentions : List ATypeExpr, Integer, Text -> Boolean
  zig-atype-list-mentions (xs) (i) (n) =
   if i >= list-length xs then False
   else if zig-atype-mentions (list-at xs i) n then True
   else zig-atype-list-mentions xs (i + 1) n

  zig-atype-mentions-any : ATypeExpr, List Name, Integer -> Boolean
  zig-atype-mentions-any (te) (tparams) (i) =
   if i >= list-length tparams then False
   else if zig-atype-mentions te ((list-at tparams i).value) then True
   else zig-atype-mentions-any te tparams (i + 1)

  zig-tparam-index : List Name, Text, Integer -> Integer
  zig-tparam-index (ps) (n) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ps then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at ps i).value == n then i
   else zig-tparam-index ps n (i + 1)

  zig-rec-type-args : CodexType -> List CodexType
  zig-rec-type-args (ty) =
   when ty
    is SumTy (n) (a) (c) -> a
    is RecordTy (n) (a) (f) -> a
    is ConstructedTy (n) (a) -> a
    is otherwise -> []

  zig-elem-atype-text : ATypeExpr, List Name, CodexType, ZigCtx -> Text
  zig-elem-atype-text (te) (tparams) (rty) (ctx) =
   when te
    is ANamedType (nm) (s) ->
     let ti = zig-tparam-index tparams (nm.value) 0
     in if ti < 0 then emit-zig-atype te
        else let actuals = zig-rec-type-args rty
        in if ti >= list-length actuals then ""
           else emit-zig-type (list-at actuals ti) (ctx.scope-tvars)
    is otherwise ->
     if zig-atype-mentions-any te tparams 0 then ""
     else emit-zig-atype te

  zig-atype-ll-elem : ATypeExpr, List Name, CodexType, ZigCtx -> Text
  zig-atype-ll-elem (te) (tparams) (rty) (ctx) =
   when te
    is AAppType (base) (args) (s) ->
     when base
      is ANamedType (bn) (bs) ->
       if (bn.value == "List") | (bn.value == "LinkedList")
       then zig-elem-atype-text (list-at args 0) tparams rty ctx
       else ""
      is otherwise -> ""
    is otherwise -> ""

  zig-record-field-ll-elem : ZigCtx, Text, Text, CodexType -> Text
  zig-record-field-ll-elem (ctx) (recname) (fieldname) (rty) =
   zig-record-field-ll-scan (ctx.typedefs) 0 recname fieldname rty ctx

  zig-record-field-ll-scan : List ATypeDef, Integer, Text, Text, CodexType, ZigCtx -> Text
  zig-record-field-ll-scan (tds) (i) (recname) (fieldname) (rty) (ctx) =
   if i >= list-length tds then ""
   else when (list-at tds i)
    is ARecordTypeDef (name) (tparams) (fields) (recmut) (s) ->
     if name.value == recname then zig-field-ll-scan fields 0 fieldname tparams rty ctx
     else zig-record-field-ll-scan tds (i + 1) recname fieldname rty ctx
    is otherwise -> zig-record-field-ll-scan tds (i + 1) recname fieldname rty ctx

  zig-field-ll-scan : List ARecordFieldDef, Integer, Text, List Name, CodexType, ZigCtx -> Text
  zig-field-ll-scan (fields) (i) (fieldname) (tparams) (rty) (ctx) =
   if i >= list-length fields then ""
   else let f = list-at fields i
   in if f.name.value == fieldname then zig-atype-ll-elem (f.type-expr) tparams rty ctx
      else zig-field-ll-scan fields (i + 1) fieldname tparams rty ctx

 The callee's declared parameter type, translated through whatever
 bindings this call resolved -- a declaration read in the caller names the
 callee's type variables until it is substituted.

  zig-callee-param-type : ZigCtx, Text, Integer -> CodexType
  zig-callee-param-type (ctx) (fname) (i) =
   let di = zig-find-irdef (ctx.irdefs) 0 fname
   in if di < 0 then VoidTy
   else let d = list-at (ctx.irdefs) di
   in if i >= list-length (d.params) then VoidTy
      else zig-subst-ctx ((list-at (d.params) i).type-val) ctx

  zig-param-ll-elem : ZigCtx, Text, Integer -> Text
  zig-param-ll-elem (ctx) (fname) (argi) =
   zig-param-ll-scan (ctx.irdefs) 0 fname argi ctx

  zig-param-ll-scan : List IRDef, Integer, Text, Integer, ZigCtx -> Text
  zig-param-ll-scan (defs) (i) (fname) (argi) (ctx) =
   if i >= list-length defs then ""
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in if d.name == fname then
    (if argi >= list-length (d.params) then ""
     else let pty = zig-subst-ctx ((list-at (d.params) argi).type-val) ctx
     in when pty
      is ListTy (elem) -> emit-zig-type elem (ctx.scope-tvars)
      is LinkedListTy (elem) -> emit-zig-type elem (ctx.scope-tvars)
      is otherwise -> "")
   else zig-param-ll-scan defs (i + 1) fname argi ctx

 RECORDS have reference semantics on bare metal -- all of them, not
 the ones spelled `mutable`: emit-record-set-builtin stores through the
 record's pointer and returns that pointer, and a field store mutates
 the object every holder sees. The zig emission mirrors that by
 declaring the struct as <Name>S and making the bare name an alias for
 *<Name>S, so every type reference is a pointer and no set of
 privileged type names is threaded anywhere.

  zig-in-texts : List Text, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  zig-in-texts (xs) (n) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then False
   else if list-at xs i == n then True
   else zig-in-texts xs n (i + 1)

 Records are pointers without exception now, so a field store is
 always a store THROUGH one and never a copy-modify-return, and the
 question zig-is-mutable-rec used to answer has no askers left.

  variant-all-nullary : List AVariantCtorDef, Integer -> Boolean
  variant-all-nullary (ctors) (i) =
   if i == list-length ctors then True
   else if list-length ((list-at ctors i).fields) > 0 then False
   else variant-all-nullary ctors (i + 1)

  build-ctor-map : List ATypeDef, Integer, List ZigCtorEntry -> List ZigCtorEntry
  build-ctor-map (tds) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length tds then acc
   else let td = list-at tds i
   in when td
    is AVariantTypeDef (name) (tparams) (ctors) (s) ->
     build-ctor-map tds (i + 1) (build-ctor-entries (name.value) ctors (variant-all-nullary ctors 0) 0 acc)
    is otherwise -> build-ctor-map tds (i + 1) acc

  build-ctor-entries : Text, List AVariantCtorDef, Boolean, Integer, List ZigCtorEntry -> List ZigCtorEntry
  build-ctor-entries (tname) (ctors) (enum-like) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length ctors then acc
   else let c = list-at ctors i
   in build-ctor-entries tname ctors enum-like (i + 1) (list-push acc (ZigCtorEntry {
    ctor-name = c.name.value,
    type-name = tname,
    ctor-arity = __narrow (list-length (c.fields)),
    enum-like = enum-like
   }))

  zig-find-ctor : List ZigCtorEntry, Text, Integer -> Integer
  zig-find-ctor (cs) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length cs then -1
   else if (list-at cs i).ctor-name == name then i
   else zig-find-ctor cs name (i + 1)

Section: Builtin Emitter

  ZigBuiltinEmitter = record {
   name : Text,
   emit : List IRExpr, ZigCtx, Integer, CodexType -> Text
  }

  bsearch-zig-builtin-pos : List ZigBuiltinEmitter, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bsearch-zig-builtin-pos (entries) (name) (lo) (hi) =
   if lo >= hi then lo
   else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
   in let mid-name = (list-at entries mid).name
   in if text-compare name mid-name <= 0 then bsearch-zig-builtin-pos entries name lo mid
      else bsearch-zig-builtin-pos entries name (mid + 1) hi

  sort-zig-builtins : List ZigBuiltinEmitter -> List ZigBuiltinEmitter
  sort-zig-builtins (xs) = sort-by xs (\a b -> text-compare (a.name) (b.name))

  zig-ll-elem : CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Text
  zig-ll-elem (ty) (scope) =
   let e = zig-ll-elem-opt ty scope
   in if text-length e > 0 then e else "@compileError(\"zig plug: no element type for this list\")"

 The annotation is not always a list type -- a type variable reaches here
 unresolved -- so this answers "" for that rather than a default, and the
 callers that have another source say so explicitly.

  zig-ll-elem-opt : CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Text
  zig-ll-elem-opt (ty) (scope) =
   when ty
    is ListTy (elem) -> emit-zig-type elem scope
    is LinkedListTy (elem) -> emit-zig-type elem scope
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (inner) -> zig-ll-elem-opt inner scope
    is otherwise -> ""

 An IrList's own annotation is already the element type, so it renders
 directly. It answers "" for the two types that carry no information --
 an unresolved variable and the void a missing annotation becomes -- so
 the callers' own fallbacks still run.

  zig-elem-annotation : CodexType, List (Integer between 0 and 4294967295) -> Text
  zig-elem-annotation (ty) (scope) =
   when ty
    is VoidTy -> ""
    is TypeVar (vid) -> ""
    is otherwise -> emit-zig-type ty scope

 __record-set names its field with a text literal, so the field is known
 here and becomes a real field access rather than anything dynamic. The
 record is copied and the field replaced: for a value record that yields a
 new value, and for a mutable record -- which this emitter represents as a
 pointer -- copying the pointer and storing through it mutates in place,
 which is what bare metal does. A non-literal field name cannot be
 compiled at all, so it becomes a zig compile error rather than a guess.

  zig-record-set-field : IRExpr -> Text
  zig-record-set-field (e) =
   when e
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> zig-field-name s
    is otherwise -> "@compileError(\"__record-set needs a literal field name\")"

  zig-record-set-plain-field : IRExpr -> Text
  zig-record-set-plain-field (e) =
   when e
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> zig-plain-field-name s
    is otherwise -> ""

  zig-record-set : List IRExpr, ZigCtx, Integer -> Text
  zig-record-set (args) (ctx) (d) =
   let tmp = "_r" & integer-to-text d
   in let rec-ty = zig-expr-type (list-at args 0)
   in let rec-name = zig-type-name-of rec-ty
   in let field-name = zig-record-set-plain-field (list-at args 1)
   in zig-label d & ": { const " & tmp & " = " & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 0) ctx (d + 1)
    & "; " & tmp & "." & zig-record-set-field (list-at args 1)
    & " = " & zig-field-value-text rec-name field-name (list-at args 2) ctx d rec-ty
    & "; break :" & zig-label d & " " & tmp & "; }"

 Text is CCE in the interior -- on bare metal, in the C# plug and here --
 because CCE is codex semantics and not a runtime encoding choice: `E` IS
 39, and CCE orders letters by frequency, so a plug holding UTF-8 would
 disagree with bare metal on every comparison, ordering and substring
 index. A target's own encoding appears at I/O and nowhere else, which for
 this plug means cx_cce_to_utf8 on the way to the terminal.

 A Char is a CCE CODE here, the identity model bare metal and the C# plug
 share: char-code and code-to-char vanish, char-at and char-code-at are the
 same bare read, and a char literal's raw IR payload -- already a CCE code
 -- compares equal to what char-at reads. Classification is banded in CCE
 (cx_is_letter, cx_is_digit). The codepoint alternative was tried and is
 structurally lossy: CCE has aliases (a tier-1 code and a tier-0 code can
 name the same codepoint, and the canonical encoder picks tier 0), so any
 code -> codepoint -> code detour canonicalises and corrupts byte-wise
 text rebuilds. Codepoints exist at the I/O boundary only.

  zig-builtin-emitters : List ZigBuiltinEmitter =
   sort-zig-builtins [
    ZigBuiltinEmitter { name = "__record-set", emit = \args ctx d ty -> zig-record-set args ctx d },
    ZigBuiltinEmitter { name = "text-starts-with", emit = \args ctx d ty -> "cx_text_starts_with(" & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 0) ctx (d + 1) & ", " & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 1) ctx (d + 1) & ")" },
    ZigBuiltinEmitter { name = "text-contains", emit = \args ctx d ty -> "cx_text_contains(" & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 0) ctx (d + 1) & ", " & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 1) ctx (d + 1) & ")" },
    ZigBuiltinEmitter { name = "text-concat-list", emit = \args ctx d ty -> "cx_text_concat_list(" & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 0) ctx (d + 1) & ")" },
    ZigBuiltinEmitter { name = "text-to-integer", emit = \args ctx d ty -> "cx_text_to_integer(" & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 0) ctx (d + 1) & ")" },
    ZigBuiltinEmitter { name = "text-split", emit = \args ctx d ty -> "cx_text_split(" & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 0) ctx (d + 1) & ", " & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 1) ctx (d + 1) & ")" },
    ZigBuiltinEmitter { name = "text-replace", emit = \args ctx d ty -> "cx_text_replace(" & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 0) ctx (d + 1) & ", " & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 1) ctx (d + 1) & ", " & emit-zig-expr (list-at args 2) ctx (d + 1) & ")" },
